*.swo

# OS
Thumbs.db

# Logs
logs/
*.log

# EAS
.eas/
# Google Play service-account key for `eas submit` — never commit.
google-play-service-account.json

# FCM v1 service-account key (MEAL-225) — never commit. This is the one that can
# SEND to every device we have a token for, and Firebase downloads it with a
# generated name like `mealio-app-firebase-adminsdk-abc12-0123456789.json`, so
# the pattern matters more than any one filename.
#
# NOT the same file as `google-services.json`, which IS committed and belongs in
# the repo: that one carries the project and app ids that ship inside every APK
# anyway, and EAS needs it present at build time. Confusing the two is the whole
# reason both are named here.
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
firebase-service-account*.json

# google-services.json — OUT of the repo, and the reasoning is worth keeping.
#
# Google documents the Android API key inside it as safe to embed, and that is
# true: it ships in every APK and anyone can extract it. But this repository is
# PUBLIC, GitHub's scanner flags it on every push, and an alert nobody can
# action is how a real one comes to be ignored. It reaches EAS as a file-type
# secret (`GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON`) instead; app.config.js reads that path.
#
# It is still not the secret one. That is the *firebase-adminsdk* pattern above,
# which can push to every device we hold a token for.
google-services.json

# Test infrastructure — never commit these.
# NOTE: git does not support inline comments in .gitignore. A trailing
# comment becomes part of the pattern and the rule silently fails to match.
# Persistent Chrome profiles for capture/live tests (contain login cookies):
tests/.chrome-profile/
# Unencrypted credentials:
tests/live/creds.json

# Claude Code personal settings (commands/ and MCP-SETUP.md ARE committed)
.claude/settings.local.json

# Personal running backlog — maintained locally, not tracked in git.
TODO.md
TODO-DONE.md

# Playwright MCP scratch output — page snapshots written during debugging.
.playwright-mcp/
